# Operational specification for the forecasting model stack

October 9, 2026. This specification fills statistical handoffs in the [architecture](model-stack-design-20261008.md) and applies the [marketing research audit](marketing-research-audit-20261009.md). It defines selected research models and experiments, not completed fitting or forecast superiority. The implementation session owns execution.

## 1. Selected inference target

The initial research target is **population_cut_v1**, with the route-specific coordinates and evidence owners in the [selected ownership contract](handoffs-20261009/cut-contract-proposal.md). Write the protected state as \(U_r\), and let \(C,X\) denote realized publisher calibration controls and recorded design inputs:

\[
p_{\rm cut}(U_r,\Psi\mid D_N,D_S,D_R,D_B,C,X)
=p(U_r\mid D_N)\,q(\Psi\mid U_r,D_N,D_S,D_R,D_B,C,X).
\]

External demographic/geographic evidence \(D_N\) determines the protected population and boundary distribution and all its source-error/dynamics hyperparameters. The selected exposure state is \(U_{\rm exp}=(E,R,H,J,\eta_N)\); it has no finite eligibility coordinate. \(\Psi\) jointly contains community prevalence, selection, response signatures, fundamentals, environment, participation and candidate response. \(D_S\) contains admitted surveys/polls; \(D_R\) contains historical participation and returns; \(D_B\) contains separately owned bridge/linkage evidence. These sets do not duplicate records. The second factor is normalized separately for each protected state, with a finite positive normalizer, not a product of separately fitted layer posteriors. The ownership contract gives the kernel and shared-evidence conditions.

For the exposure count law, the [reviewed event-parent clarification](handoffs-20261010/event-report-composition-root-v1.md) records that the later selected downstream v3/full-cut law uses one Gamma frailty across a complete event. It supersedes the frozen ownership proposal's geographic-parent `U_ge` count wording. Protected-state ownership and separate downstream normalization under that contract remain unchanged.

Recorded \(C\) remains what the publisher used, even when latent population draws differ. A control derived from \(D_N\) is not another likelihood row. Shared stochastic errors require the declared conditional observation law; the initial build gate rejects unsupported cross-cut dependence rather than assuming it away. A future supported finite route assigns eligibility \(N_E,\zeta\) downstream under its own versioned contract. None of these decisions changes an already frozen run.

This allows polls to update community prevalence while protecting demographic totals from political-model misspecification. It does not identify prevalence separately from selection and signatures: the bridge/sensitivity tests below remain necessary. **composition_cut_v1**, described in architecture section 12, protects affiliation prevalence as well and is a comparator. **full_joint_v1** also permits political evidence to update demographics. Each gets a separate target ID and cache identity. These choices adapt [Carmona and Nicholls' cut framework](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v108/carmona20a/carmona20a.pdf).

External affiliation surveys require their own selection/measurement nuisance parameters. They cannot be treated as error-free population measurements. Every nuisance parameter and shared hyperparameter has one owner and declared conditioning path.

Use one joint factor per respondent/sample, integrating missing answers rather than treating imputations as new observations. Linked fields, repeated waves and shared survey crosstabs retain dependence. Historical results used to discover a representation remain in their one likelihood, or enter a declared cross-fitting approximation. When explicitly modeled sample outcomes contribute to an official total, condition the aggregate on those contributions and model the remainder. Small-sample conditional independence is an approximation requiring sensitivity checks.

The [integrated downstream selection](handoffs-20261009/downstream-joint-component-selection-v1.md), [v3 contract](handoffs-20261009/downstream-joint-component-proposal-v3.md) and [independent review](handoffs-20261009/downstream-joint-component-proposal-v3-independent-review.md) now select the conditional experimental composition of profile, polling, behavior, applicability, count and report laws. Its source/design operators, per-parent normalizers, full-cut moments, inference and predictive qualification remain separate obligations. The selection is conditional on valid supplied inputs, not an admission or fitted model. The [current component disposition](handoffs-20261009/component-milestone-root-disposition-v1.md) links bounded independent behavior, applicability and supplied-state population-storage qualifications; the [earlier build audit](handoffs-20261009/build-integration-milestone-audit-v5.md) retains its dated scope. None is a complete empirical stack qualification.

The [selected empirical test design](handoffs-20261009/empirical-test-design-selection-v1.md), with [independent review](handoffs-20261009/empirical-test-protocol-independent-review-v1.md), now fixes four matched arms, information and issuance deadlines, truth snapshots and common score scales for the full 35/51 forecast target. The [effective contract v5](handoffs-20261009/empirical-test-contract-v5.json) pairs with protocol v4; newer unused draft filenames do not override that selection. The [conditional next-source candidates](handoffs-20261009/next-observation-validation-root-review-v1.md) separately specify resident-adult and national survey validation opportunities, with unadmitted measurement bridges. The [denominator preparation delta](handoffs-20261009/build-integration-milestone-audit-v4.md) supplements the preceding bounded build audit.

